body.proof_tex:
Consider one frozen support row \(z=(z_1,z_2,z_3)\in E\), its repaired row \(z'=z+\delta\), and define
\[
 S=\sum_a z_a,\quad D=\sum_a\delta_a,\quad
 g_a=-2\sum_{b\ne a}z_b=-2(S-z_a).
\]
The definition of \(G_{as}\) gives \(g_a\in G_a(z_a)\).  Dual tightness at \(z\) gives \(S^2=\sum_a u_a(z_a)\), and \(u_a(r)=r^2-\phi_a(r)\).  Direct expansion, using this equality in the last step, gives the exact identity
\[
\begin{aligned}
 C(z')-\sum_a u_a(z'_a)
 &= (S+D)^2-\sum_a(z_a+\delta_a)^2+\sum_a\phi_a(z'_a)\\
 &=D^2-\sum_a\delta_a^2
   +\sum_a\{\phi_a(z'_a)-\phi_a(z_a)-g_a\delta_a\}. \tag{16}
\end{aligned}
\]
This is a dual slack because the dual inequalities hold at every atom triple, including the repaired triple.

Cauchy--Schwarz in three coordinates yields
\[
 D^2\le3\sum_a\delta_a^2,\quad
 D^2-\sum_a\delta_a^2\le2\sum_a\delta_a^2. \tag{17}
\]
If a nonzero arm-\(a\) movement has lowest common partition block at depth \(\ell\), its source and target intervals lie in one arm-\(a\) grid cell of width \(h_{a,\ell}\).  Lemma lem:ordered-dual-slopes, applied with the actual source support slope \(g_a\), therefore gives
\[
 \delta_a^2\le h_{a,\ell}^2,\quad
 0\le\phi_a(z'_a)-\phi_a(z_a)-g_a\delta_a
 \le h_{a,\ell}^2. \tag{18}
\]
An unmoved coordinate contributes zero.  Combining (16)--(18), each moved coordinate at that depth is charged at most \(3h_{a,\ell}^2\).

Sum over the \(N\) frozen rows.  The repair preserves each marginal multiplicity.  If \(b_{as}=NF_a(\{s\})\), dual optimality for \(q^\star=N\pi^\star\) and Definition def:fractional-value imply
\[
 \sum_{\mathrm{rows}}\sum_a u_a(z'_a)
 =\sum_{a,s}b_{as}u_{as}
 =N V_{\mathrm{rel}}(F). \tag{19}
\]
Using the movement bound (15) from Lemma lem:hierarchical-repair in (16) gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
 N\{C_{\mathrm{alg}}(F)-V_{\mathrm{rel}}(F)\}
 &\le3\sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1}\sum_{a=1}^3h_{a,\ell}^2M_{a,\ell}\\
 &\le3\sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1}\sum_{a=1}^3h_{a,\ell}^2R_{\ell+1}. \tag{20}
\end{aligned}
\]
By (7),
\[
 \sum_{a=1}^3h_{a,\ell}^2
 =\left\{2\left(\frac74\right)^2+\left(\frac32\right)^2\right\}4^{-\ell}
 =\frac{67}{8}4^{-\ell}. \tag{21}
\]
Insert the residual bound (12) into (20), and extend the finite sum by positive terms.  Since
\[
 \sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}\left(12\,2^{-\ell}-6\,4^{-\ell}\right)
 =24-8=16, \tag{22}
\]
equations (20)--(22) yield
\[
\begin{aligned}
 N\{C_{\mathrm{alg}}(F)-V_{\mathrm{rel}}(F)\}
 &\le3\sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1}\sum_a h_{a,\ell}^2R_{\ell+1}\\
 &<3\left\{2\left(\frac74\right)^2+\left(\frac32\right)^2\right\}
 \sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}\left(12\,2^{-\ell}-6\,4^{-\ell}\right)\\
 &=3\cdot\frac{67}{8}\cdot16=402. \tag{23}
\end{aligned}
\]
The strict inequality follows because \(L\) is finite and every omitted summand in (22) is positive.  If \(L=0\), no repair is needed and the finite sums are empty.  This proves both the identity and the universal bound.
